{"title":"2009 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 11","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Diagram_(with_lengths)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Diagram (with lengths)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Overcomplicated)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Overcomplicated)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(answer_choices)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (answer choices)</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2009_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The perimeter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/6/3b6d5d23a7e91fb9f6c248fb4cd43111165540f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta ABC$\" width=\"57\" height=\"13\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/4/4f4b8590f8aa9f9894b3891dd5dede0e354fa04e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$32$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/a/18aa6e9e7aee82c1f816c62fa672809b4c64bf55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ABC = \\angle ACB$\" width=\"134\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/f/d5fb59fff6b6968adfa1e4b3d06a609a2a8af375.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC = 12$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"70\" height=\"13\" />, the length of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/2/c928733eeb034947fae4c578baeef8b570155f15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{ (A) }\\ 11 \\qquad\\text{ (B) }\\ 10 \\qquad\\text{ (C) }\\ 9 \\qquad\\text{ (D) }\\ 12 \\qquad\\text{ (E) }\\ 8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"415\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Diagram_.28with_lengths.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Diagram_(with_lengths)\">Diagram (with lengths)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2009_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Diagram (with lengths)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/d/52d79f51e79e7430ca9b810dd99da020e6e35cc6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(200);  pair A = (0,0); pair B = (12,0); pair C = (6,8);  draw(A--B--C--cycle);  label(&quot;$A$&quot;, A, SW); label(&quot;$B$&quot;, B, SE); label(&quot;$C$&quot;, C, N);  label(&quot;12&quot;, midpoint(A--B), S); label(&quot;10&quot;, midpoint(B--C), NE); label(&quot;10&quot;, midpoint(A--C), NW);  // optional altitude (because why not) draw((6,0)--(6,8), dashed);  [/asy]\" width=\"335\" height=\"238\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2009_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/a/18aa6e9e7aee82c1f816c62fa672809b4c64bf55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ABC = \\angle ACB$\" width=\"134\" height=\"13\" />, the triangle is an <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Isosceles_triangle\" title=\"Isosceles triangle\">isosceles triangle</a>, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/a/f3ac37c9fa391eebb9601bfaca8bfbfaabdf5ca1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB = AC$\" width=\"80\" height=\"13\" />. That means that we can let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> represent <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>The perimeter is the sum of the side lengths of a polygon, meaning we can set up an equation:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/f/e3f9fd51952a978cc2ce9e836873a7f157ee6add.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x + x + 12 = 32$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"128\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/9/599f29bf909c7c10dd53e7524edbbf22d54ad49a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2x + 12 = 32$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"104\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/f/37f41a556a072eeec7fcc924aca00068a091214a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2x = 20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"64\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/5/835284442381427628a40ffd2ab5e335cbf81ab0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\boxed {\\textbf {(B) } 10}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"101\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Overcomplicated.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Overcomplicated)\">Solution 2 (Overcomplicated)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2009_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Overcomplicated)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Seeing an isosceles triangle might remind you of triangle ratio formulas and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Stewart%27s_Theorem\" title=\"Stewart&#39;s Theorem\">Stewart's Theorem</a>. Drop an altitude from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> and call the point it meets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />. This splits the triangle into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> congruent triangles. Because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/6/816beb1dc6e9feb897721c321fa3344f60539b2f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC=12$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"70\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/0/f30dcb3bcb3b38b0f6de7c8d0db06979d896bedc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BX=CX=6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"121\" height=\"14\" />. Call the altitude <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/8/2d857b2c804701b561a5c8c90c400f360d27232c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AX$\" width=\"29\" height=\"13\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />. Now, we can plug these values into Stewart's Theorem: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/8/6b850645f95350e36e9b038e9496f3db7b9702ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$man+d^{2}a=b^{2}m+c^{2}n \\rightarrow man+dad=bmb+cnc$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"411\" height=\"18\" />. (man and dad put bomb in sink) <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/4/8240e842e8421b1a800446f9e2d304fbacbf4532.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\rightarrow b^{2}6+b^{2}6=12(8^{2}+36)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"209\" height=\"21\" /> solving gets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/0/570c1029d87bc79362cb97bc41f1ec904fd7c954.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed {\\textbf {(B) } 10}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"66\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28answer_choices.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(answer_choices)\">Solution 3 (answer choices)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2009_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (answer choices)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can test answer choices, and see whether or not the side length results in the perimeter being too high or too low.\n</p><p><br />\nWe can first use <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, and we get:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/6/7f677bb72c5bccf5f7a94d2aceaaa98adcb65b51.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10 + 10 + 12 = 20 + 12 = 32$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"226\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>This happens to be exactly the perimeter of the triangle, so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/0/570c1029d87bc79362cb97bc41f1ec904fd7c954.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed {\\textbf {(B) } 10}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"66\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2009_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)\" title=\"2009 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)\">2009 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2009_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2009 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2009_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2009 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.ph